papers.summary:
The fullest statement of the school's dark sector in this set (proceedings of a September 2023 conference, posted July 2024; it overlaps BHM 2023, Symmetry). New relative to the 2020–2021 statements the record quotes:
- dark-matter fields as backgrounds of the dual momentum p̃;
- dark matter 'fuzzy' because visible and dual fields do not commute;
- a leading-order effective action with no written cross term, in which integrating over x̃ fixes G_N and gives a positive Λ;
- a dark–visible correlation 'via the minimal length λ' proposed as the origin of a₀ ~ cH/(2π);
- a dual SM from the other E8, CP phases from the dual SM, and dark-sector parameters with indeterminacy reciprocal to the SM's;
- only here: a Berry-phase-like mixing whose scale M_SM ≈ 4.5×10^14 GeV is a decoupling energy below which dark and visible matter interact only gravitationally.
The spacetime polarization is selected by quantum gravity through extensification (the pointer basis at large N), not spontaneously. x̃ is both the rescaled momentum of one phase space (modular polarization) and an independent coordinate of a doubled phase space (metaparticle), at scales l_P, ℓ_s or l_eff.

Conference paper (April 2026) that restates 2407.06207 §3 nearly word for word. Additions:
- x is 'canonically conjugate to dual spacetime x̃';
- fixing the spacetime polarization recovers local QFT;
- the dual Higgs might drive Starobinsky-like inflation, and the axion's dual partner might be fuzzy dark matter (speculative, partly as questions);
- a mechanism for a₀ (fuzzy dark matter's quantum pressure, sensitive to the vacuum energy), and the claim that the observed acceleration scale is evidence for the dark–visible correlation;
- time-varying dark energy contemplated (citing Hur et al. 2025);
- quantum gravity picks the spacetime basis, and quantum gravity should have both a dynamical polarization (in QM's sense, ψ → Uψ) and a dynamical geometry.
It does not repeat the 2024 decoupling scale and does not retract it.

answers.answer:
Both, in the same papers, with nothing separating the two uses.
(a) The canonical conjugate, on one phase space. In the modular polarization of ordinary quantum mechanics, x̃ is the rescaled canonical momentum: 'x≡q/λ and x̃≡p/ε and λε=ħ', so '[x,x̃]=i' (2407.06207 §3.2–§3.3; 2604.19418 §5.1). Minic 2023 calls the momenta p 'dual coordinates conjugate to x', with [x̂, p̂] = i. 2610.19522 §3.2 derives X̃ as Y, the coordinate that 'naturally lives in momentum space', writes the symplectic form as (1/ħ) dx̃∧dx, and says ω is 'encoded in the canonical commutator between x and x̃ (or the canonical commutator between q and p in quantum theory)'. This is #11's single phase space with ω rigid at ħ.
(b) An independent coordinate of a doubled phase space. For the metaparticle and the metastring, on which every dark-matter statement rests: '[x,x̃]=iλ², ... [p,p̃]=0 ... [x,p]=iħ=[x̃,p̃]' (2407.06207 §3.3; 2604.19418 §5.1; 2610.19522 §3.2). 2604.19418 calls x 'canonically conjugate to dual spacetime x̃', and in §7 'conjugate, in the sense of canonical commutation relations'. Executed check: the metaparticle Poisson brackets (2407.06207 eq. 3.15) have rank 4 per dimension, so x̃ is not a function of (x, p); the modular change of variables has rank 2.
Scales: λ is 'contextual'.
- λ² = ħα′ (Minic 2023);
- ℓ_s (2407.06207 §3.5, §5.1; 2610.19522 eq. 3.10);
- l_P (2407.06207 §3.2 and §4.3; 2604.19418 §5.2). The same heuristic chain gives ℓ_s² in 2610.19522;
- l_eff (2407.06207 §3.5).
Normalizations vary: iλ², iπλ² (Minic 2023), 2πiλ² (2610.19522 eq. 3.3), 2πiℓ_s² (2407.06207 §5.1), πα′ (eq. 3.15). One more source of the non-commutativity is named: 'a constant and nonzero Kalb-Ramond B-field' (2407.06207 §5.1). Dark fields are backgrounds of p̃ ('p→p+φ and p̃→p̃+φ̃').

Not found in any of the papers: self-interaction, dependence on relative velocity, a momentum-overlap gate, or localization in x̃ or momentum ('self-interact' occurs 0 times; 'velocit' only in unrelated contexts).
The form given is 'fuzzy dark matter', from the non-commutativity of φ and φ̃ (2407.06207 §3.3–§3.4; 2604.19418 throughout; 'necessarily fuzzy, and thus, field-like', 2604.19418 §7). It is tied to quantum pressure and the vacuum energy (2604.19418 §5.1) and to Edmonds, Erlich, Minic, Takeuchi 2024 (arXiv:2405.08744).
Other dynamical statements:
- a dual particle at energy μ/E for each particle at energy E (2407.06207 eq. 3.7);
- a 'third quantization indeterminacy' of the dark fields, with dark-sector parameters whose indeterminacy is 'reciprocal' to the SM's precision (2407.06207 §6);
- the dual Higgs as Starobinsky-like inflation and the axion's dual partner as fuzzy dark matter (2604.19418, speculative, the latter partly a question);
- Vafa's decaying-dark-matter scale, reported for M_SM (2407.06207 §5.3; Vafa's postulate, not the authors' claim).
The dual spacetime 'looks completely invisible' once the spacetime polarization is picked (2604.19418 §7). Visible fermions are described topologically, as unit flux through modular cells (2407.06207 §3.4) or 'defects or kinks' (2610.19522 §3.2), but nothing of the kind is said of dark matter.
My comparison: there is nothing like #11 §8's velocity gate (the χ form). The school's fuzzy form is a wave form, nearer #11's wave-dark-matter and X-condensate lines.

Dark energy is the curvature of the dual spacetime. At leading order in λ, integrating R̃(x̃) over x̃ 'produces a positive cosmological constant' (2407.06207 §3.4; 2604.19418 §5.1). Other statements:
- 'Metaparticles appear as natural dark matter quanta and the geometry of dual spacetime as the natural origin of dark energy' (2407.06207 §3.1);
- dark energy, 'modeled as the cosmological constant', is 'the curvature of the dual spacetime, and naturally small' (§6);
- 2604.19418 adds 'In general one can contemplate a time-varying dark energy [11]' (Hur et al. 2025, DESI) and 'a cosmological constant plus a possible time variable part ... with observable implications for time dependent vacuum energy' (§7); it proposes the dark-energy scale as the scale for laboratory tests of gravitized quantum theory;
- 2610.19522: 'curvature of dual spacetime can lead to a dynamical origin of dark energy', and neutrinos as 'portals' to the dark-energy sector.
These six texts give no w value, no phantom statement, and no contraction or collapse of a dual universe (grep). Minic 2023's rescaling x → ax, p → a⁻¹p, with the dual dimension shrinking as a → ∞, is kinematics of the modular cell used as a decoupling limit; it parallels #11's a·ã = 1 (App. A item 6) but describes no evolution in time.
Against #11 §10: the program's dark energy is τ (hilltop quintessence on the Maslov circle, w ≥ −1). Its corrected dictionary maps dual curvature to negative dark radiation and dual stiff matter to Λ, whereas these papers make dual curvature the cosmological constant. The constructions differ (x̃-integration of a doubled-space action, versus a dual-frame fluid under a·ã = 1 with one lapse), so this contrasts two claims rather than testing either (my reading). Against C5 ('their collapse is our acceleration'): nothing in these papers.

None of my papers is the first statement, and all cite earlier papers of the school for it:
- Minic 2023 cites [10]: BHM arXiv:2212.06086, with arXiv:2111.14205, LHEP 2021, PLB 798 (2019) and IJMPD 28 (2019);
- 2407.06207 cites LHEP 2021 [49, 103], BHM 2212.06086 [9], FKGLM 2019 (metaparticles) [42] and BHM 2021 Universe [104];
- 2604.19418 cites LHEP 2021, arXiv:2111.14205 and 2212.06086;
- 2610.19522 cites Minic 2020 (arXiv:2003.00318) [27] and LHEP 2021 [70].
None cites anyone outside the school for the identification.
I checked the earliest items they cite:
- the 2019 BHM papers (arXiv:1905.08269, PLB 798, 134950; arXiv:1905.09463, IJMPD 28, 1944018) mention dark matter only in 'our discussion naturally relates to the observationally supported proposal for dark matter quanta that are sensitive to dark energy', citing Edmonds et al. 2017;
- 'Theory of metaparticles' (FKGLM, arXiv:1812.10821, v1 2018-12-27) has no occurrence of 'dark'.
In the citation chains of these papers, Minic 2020 therefore remains the earliest statement, consistent with the record.
Dates against the program's notebook v0.1 (September 2026): 2203.17137 (2022-03-31) through 2604.19418 (2026-04-21), and the unread book (2026-06-04 online; INSPIRE 2026-07-21), are earlier; 2610.19522 (2026-10-14) is later, a restatement.

How the statement develops across these papers:
- March 2022 (GRF essay): nothing on dark matter.
- March/June 2023 (CQG): nothing on dark matter; x̃ only as non-commuting dual coordinates.
- May 2023 (Minic talk; journal 2024): one parenthesis. Metaparticles are correlated visible/dual pairs, 'natural candidates'; the dual sector drops out in the b → ∞ decoupling limit.
- July 2024 (Hübsch–Minic): the fullest account:
  - dark fields as backgrounds of the dual momentum p̃, fuzzy through [φ, φ̃] ≠ 0;
  - an effective action in which the x̃-integration gives G_N and Λ;
  - the λ-correlation proposed as the origin of a₀ ~ cH/(2π);
  - a dual SM from the other E8, CP phases from it, and reciprocal indeterminacy of its parameters;
  - a decoupling energy M_SM ≈ 4.5×10^14 GeV, below which dark and visible matter interact only gravitationally;
  - the spacetime polarization selected by quantum gravity through extensification.
- April 2026: §3 repeated, adding the dual Higgs as Starobinsky-like inflation and the axion's dual partner as fuzzy dark matter (speculative); the quantum-pressure mechanism for a₀ and the claim that a₀ is evidence; time-varying dark energy; x 'canonically conjugate' to x̃; quantum gravity picking the spacetime basis. The decoupling scale is not repeated.
- June/July 2026 (book): not read.
- October 2026: the identification hedged ('a candidate', 'the potential to describe'); X̃ derived as the string's momentum-space coordinate; η named the polarization metric; neutrinos as portals to dark energy; nothing new on the dark sector's dynamics.
Across the period: no self-interaction, no velocity dependence, no monopoles, no localization in momentum, no spontaneous selection of the polarization.

contradicts_record.why:
This qualifies the contrast the summary draws. In these papers 'correlated' means three things:
- the dark field does not commute with its visible partner, at order λ;
- each particle is paired with its dual in one metaparticle ('rigid length-ℓ_s strings that correlate each Standard Model particle with its dual', 2407.06207 §5.1);
- a Berry-phase-like mixing term whose mass scale is a decoupling energy.
Below ~10^14 GeV the 2024 restatement has gravity-only interaction, like #11's P4. What remains as a difference is the non-commutative pairing, the dual SM's contribution to visible CP phases, and the claimed galaxy-scale correlation at a₀, which #11 does not have (grep: no Milgrom, MOND or a₀ in #11, #1 or the literature-check note). It is not a non-gravitational force at accessible energies. A reader of 'correlated with ours … by gravity alone' would infer a difference in forces that the 2024 restatement excludes below about 4.5×10^14 GeV.

